use feature qw(say);
my $input = '';
my $x = $input || 'guest';
my $y = $input // 'guest';
say "[$x] [$y]";
```

What does this program print? (Ignore any trailing space.)

```perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use feature qw(say);
OUTER: for my $i (1 .. 3) {
    for my $j (1 .. 3) {
        next OUTER if $j > $i;
        print "$i$j ";
    }
}
print "\n";
```

What does this program print? It prints three lines; type them separated by single spaces.

```perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use feature qw(say signatures);
no warnings qw(experimental::signatures);
sub pair ($left, $right = 'none') { return "$left/$right" }
say pair('a');
say pair('a', 'b');
my $r = eval { pair('a', 'b', 'c') } // 'died';
say $r;
```

This program searches for an even number and finds none. What does it print, and why?

```perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use feature qw(say);
sub find_even {
    for my $n (@_) { return $n if $n % 2 == 0 }
    return undef;
}
my @found = find_even(1, 3, 5);
say scalar(@found), " ", (@found ? "true" : "false");
```
